("Tom and Susan (1973): $78,400 single income in today's dollars, mortgage $10,700, health $2,100, fixed costs about $42,000, leaving $36,400.","they have about $36,400","K02: 78,400 - 42,000 = 36,400 reproduces internally. All four Tom-and-Susan figures are about 2.0-2.2x the Two-Income Trap's original values, i.e. restated to today's dollars.",['TIT1']),
("Justin and Kimberly (early 2000s): $67,800 combined, 75% more than Tom and Susan adjusted for inflation.","That's 75% more than Tom and Susan","Internally contradictory: as stated, $67,800 is 13.5% LESS than Tom's $78,400 (K02). The 75% holds only against the book's original 1970s figure (about $38,700, analyst recall); the script restated one family to today's dollars and left the other in early-2000s dollars.",['TIT1']),
("Their mortgage is $8,978, up 76%; health insurance $1,650, up 74%.","Except their mortgage is $8,978","Internally contradictory: $8,978 is below Tom's stated $10,700 and $1,650 below $2,100 (K02). The percentage increases come from the book's unrestated figures; mixing dollar bases makes the comparison wrong as narrated.",['TIT1']),
("Two cars $8,000, daycare $5,320, after-school $4,350, a 33% federal bracket vs 24%; total fixed costs $50,755.","Total fixed costs, $50,755","K02: 8,978 + 1,650 + 8,000 + 5,320 + 4,350 = 28,298 plus 33% tax on $67,800 (22,374) = 50,672, about $50,755. Reproduces approximately. The 33% and 24% are the book's average total tax rates (analyst recall), not \"federal brackets\"; a 33% federal bracket did not apply to a $67,800 couple.",['TIT1']),
("Justin and Kimberly have $800 less to spend at the end of the year than Tom and Susan.","They also have $800 less","Not reproducible from the narrated figures: 67,800 - 50,755 = $17,045 vs $36,400, a $19,355 gap (K02). The $800 is the book's comparison in its own (unrestated) dollars; the direction (less discretionary income) holds either way.",['TIT1']),
("The Two-Income Trap (2003) by Elizabeth Warren and Amelia Tyagi studied over 2,000 bankrupt families.","from a 2003 book called The Two-Income","Correct: Warren (Harvard law professor) and Warren Tyagi, 2003, drawing on the Consumer Bankruptcy Project (about 2,000 families) by analyst recall.",['TIT1']),
("Adjusted for inflation, families today spend less on clothing, appliances, food and per car than 50 years ago.","families today spend less on clothing","The book's claim for the 1970s vs early 2000s (analyst recall); not re-verified for 2026.",['TIT1']),
("Median home cost 2.4x median family income in 1970, 6.1x in 2026, and 12.5x in San Jose.","In 1970, the median home cost about 2.4","Not checked: no source named. Harvard JCHS national price-to-income ratios of about 5.0-5.6 in 2023-2024 (analyst recall) put 6.1 at the high end; the 1970 and San Jose figures are unverified.",[]),
("In 1995 single earners could borrow 4x salary with housing under 25% of pay; dual earners borrow at 28-36% of combined pay with 3% down.","four times your salary and your housing","Simplified and unsourced: the 28/36 ratios predate 1995 and applied to all borrowers; low-down-payment programmes existed earlier. The bidding-war mechanism is the book's thesis, presented as fact.",['TIT1']),
